Let $d \ge 1$ be an integer (the number of stations). Let $G$ be a $d \times d$ real matrix (the routing-information matrix), let $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be a fixed vector (the vector of netflow rates), and let $\Gamma$ be a $d \times d$ symmetric positive semidefinite matrix (the covariance matrix). Set $R := I + G$, where $I$ is the $d \times d$ identity, and let $\xi^*=(\xi^*(t))_{t\ge 0}$ be a $d$-dimensional Brownian motion with drift $-\theta$ and covariance matrix $\Gamma$, started at the origin, defined on a filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions.

Determine, for arbitrary such data $(\theta,\Gamma,G)$, whether there exists a pair of continuous adapted processes $(W^*,Y^*)$ taking values in $\mathbb{R}^d$ such that, almost surely:
(i) $W^*(t)\ge 0$ componentwise for all $t\ge 0$;
(ii) $Y^*(0)=0$ and each component $Y^*_j$ is nondecreasing;
(iii) $W^*(t)=\xi^*(t)-G\,W^*(t)+Y^*(t)$ for all $t\ge 0$, equivalently $R\,W^*(t)=\xi^*(t)+Y^*(t)$;
(iv) for each $j=1,\dots,d$, $\int_0^\infty \mathbf{1}_{\{W^*_j(s)>0\}}\,dY^*_j(s)=0$, i.e.\ $Y^*_j$ may increase only at times $t$ when $W^*_j(t)=0$.

Moreover, if such $(W^*,Y^*)$ exists, decide whether it is pathwise unique. State precise conditions on $(\theta,\Gamma,G)$ that are necessary and sufficient for existence and uniqueness.

